1. How many business out there with physical footprint (Retail) - [get this number] much more in other industries
2. The cost of bad decisions is expensive to reverse — a single failed location can cost your business up to $5 Million. 
3. It is super important to get your locations in the right place 
4. Challenges: Data Silos, and let us walk through what happens in the industry and how it affects a business
4.1 [Need to create visuals for this and get logos] Site Selection Process Today: 
4.1.1 You have data coming in from multiple sources 
        demographics / pysographics - 
                A. census (this data is at a census block group level)
                B. ESRI (this data is at Census block level)
        foot traffic data from Placer.ai (location level)
        competition - OSM (location level, but you need to extract it from my nodes)/agg data (location level)
        Road network - ESRI /custom build on python (Graph)
4.1.2 I need to combine this data for my analysis
        Do I choose ESRI? Then I need to manually bring in all my other data sources to this system
        Do I use custom python scripts? Great! Then you need to manually bring the data I need from ESRI outside of it. (Cannot move raw data outside of ESRI - So some processing happens here: Service Areas, and then extract B. If you do this, do not be surprised if you are hit with additional per seat licenses)
    Imagine you got all the data to a single platform: 
        Standardization Layer - I need to aggregate all my data at the store service area level. Computationally expensive task - Spatial Joins
        You can bring this to your custom notebooks and standardize on H3 level.
4.1.3 You run predictive modeling on your current network to understand your drivers of performance. You feature engineer for this
4.1.4 Redo this whole process for seed points (which are at least 20x the number of locations you have) and run inference
4.1.5 You have 8000 locations that you could expand to - But this doesn't make sense. You need to optimize your network, so you are not cannibalizing into your own network sales
4.1.6 In an aggressive scenario end up with 400 locations. Business users say, nope, I want to open more locations, so you keep going back and forth and land at 800 locations. 
4.1.7 Send them a list of locations in an excel for them to do. And they have no idea what data you used, cannot interact with it etc. 
Is this the process you want for a key business decision? What if your business users needed it on a map, you build a dashboard, what if they had additional questions?
5. I think the limitations are clear. Data is siloed, geospatial operations are computationally expensive, business decisions need flexibility and scenario modeling
6. Let's look at this on databricks: 



On databricks

- Connectors, MarketPlace, Connect to your ArcGIS online account to bring your data seamlessly into databricks (Still specify the different granularity of data)
- Standardize to H3 Spatial Indexing + Spatial SQL (Optimized for spark?)
- Use External API / Install a Routing Engine to run your routing
- Aggregate from H3 indices to Trade Area (Skip Spatial Joins)
- Feature Engineer - Build geographic features distances from comeptition with H3 K ring
- ML modeling - ML Flow + UC registry
- Databricks Apps + Lakebase (PostGIS, so can hold your geospatial data)
- Foundational models for your chat to talk to your data
